                <h3>What Can and Cannot Be Estimated Upfront</h3>
                <p>Understanding the inherent uncertainty in medical cost estimation helps you plan realistically. Some costs can be predicted with reasonable confidence, while others depend entirely on clinical circumstances that only become apparent during treatment.</p>

                <p><strong>Can typically be estimated accurately:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Standard room rates and typical length of stay for the procedure</li>
                    <li>Core surgical fees for the planned primary procedure</li>
                    <li>Basic implant costs if standard options are chosen</li>
                    <li>Standard post-operative medication protocols</li>
                    <li>Basic nursing and facility charges</li>
                </ul>

                <p><strong>Cannot be predicted with certainty:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Need for blood products or transfusion</li>
                    <li>Extended ICU stay if complications develop</li>
                    <li>Additional or revision procedures discovered during surgery</li>
                    <li>Premium implant upgrades if patient chooses during consultation</li>
                    <li>Extended hospitalization beyond the typical recovery period</li>
                    <li>Unplanned imaging, tests, or specialist consultations</li>
                    <li>Treatment for pre-existing conditions that affect recovery</li>
                </ul>

