            <!-- Section 3: Payment Methods -->
            <section id="section3">
                <h2>3. Payment Methods for International Patients</h2>

                <p>China's payment infrastructure differs significantly from Western countries, and understanding your options for transferring funds to Chinese hospitals is essential for smooth financial settlement. Each payment method has distinct advantages, limitations, and processing timelines that affect when and how you can settle your medical bills.</p>

                <h3>Chinese Bank Transfer</h3>
                <p>A domestic Chinese bank transfer (&#x56FD;&#x5185;&#x94F6;&#x884C;&#x8F6C;&#x8D26;) is the most straightforward method for larger hospital payments. However, this requires you to have or open a Chinese bank account, which involves identity verification (需要身份证件) and takes several business days for a foreign national.</p>

                <p>The process typically involves visiting a bank branch in person with your passport and proof of address. Major banks offering services to foreigners include ICBC, China Construction Bank, Bank of China, and HSBC China. Once you have an account, transfers within China are instant or same-day and carry minimal fees (typically &#x00A5;0-&#x00A5;50 per transfer regardless of amount).</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h4>&#x26A0; Bank Account Opening Requirements</h4>
                    <p>Chinese banks require visitors to provide their passport, a Chinese phone number (required for SMS verification), and often proof of local address (hotel booking or rental agreement). Some hospitals can assist with this process through their international department. Budget 3-5 business days for account setup if you need to open an account specifically for medical payments.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>Alipay and WeChat Pay</h3>
                <p>Alipay (&#x652F;&#x4ED8;&#x5B9D;) and WeChat Pay (&#x5FAE;&#x4FE1;&#x652F;&#x4ED8;) dominate Chinese digital payments, but their functionality for foreign visitors has historically been limited. As of recent updates, both platforms do offer tourist versions that link to foreign credit cards, though with restrictions compared to the full Chinese citizen versions.</p>

                <p>Tourist Alipay and WeChat Pay can be activated by linking a foreign Visa, Mastercard, or UnionPay card. However, daily transaction limits apply (typically &#x00A5;3,000-&#x00A5;5,000 per day for tourist versions), making them impractical for large hospital bills. They work well for smaller ancillary expenses like meals, transportation, and incidentals.</p>

                <p>For hospital payments, these apps are rarely accepted directly as a payment method for the main bill. Some private hospitals and high-end clinics may accept Alipay for the full amount, but the daily limit issue remains problematic for substantial charges.</p>

                <h3>International Wire Transfer (SWIFT)</h3>
                <p>International wire transfers via the SWIFT network are the most common payment method for large medical bills from abroad. This involves your bank sending funds in your home currency, which is converted to RMB by the receiving Chinese hospital's bank.</p>

                <p><strong>Key considerations for international wires:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Processing time:</strong> Typically 3-5 business days, though can extend to 7 days depending on correspondent bank relationships</li>
                    <li><strong>Transfer fees:</strong> Generally $25-$50 USD per transfer from the sending side, plus receiving bank fees of &#x00A5;200-&#x00A5;500</li>
                    <li><strong>Exchange rate:</strong> Banks apply their own exchange rates, which may be less favorable than market rates</li>
                    <li><strong>Reference information:</strong> You must provide the hospital with exact wire details including SWIFT code, account number, bank name, and beneficiary name</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="info-box success">
                    <h4>&#x2705; Best Practice: Initiate Early and Confirm Receipt</h4>
                    <p>For planned procedures, initiate your wire transfer at least 7-10 business days before your admission date to ensure cleared funds are available. Always request the hospital confirm receipt within 24 hours of the transfer arriving. Keep all wire confirmation receipts as proof of payment.</p>
                </div>

                <h3>Credit Cards</h3>
                <p>Credit card acceptance at Chinese hospitals is inconsistent and depends on the institution, department, and card network. Major observations:</p>

                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Visa and Mastercard</strong> are more widely accepted than American Express or Diners Club, particularly at international departments and private hospitals</li>
                    <li><strong>UnionPay</strong> (China's domestic card network) is accepted more broadly but useless for international patients without such cards</li>
                    <li><strong>Processing fees</strong> of 1-3% may be passed to the cardholder for medical transactions at some facilities</li>
                    <li><strong>Daily limits</strong> on credit cards may restrict payment for large bills</li>
                    <li><strong>Some hospitals</strong> do not accept credit cards at all, particularly for deposit payments</li>
                </ul>

                <p>Credit cards work best for smaller balances, deposits at private clinics that advertise card acceptance, and settling final bills when remaining amounts are modest. For major procedure payments, credit cards should not be your primary plan.</p>

                <h3>Cash (RMB)</h3>
                <p>Paying hospital bills in cash (&#x73B0;&#x91D1;) in Chinese Yuan remains viable, particularly at private hospitals and for smaller amounts. Some international patients bring significant USD cash and exchange it at Chinese banks upon arrival, though this requires careful security arrangements.</p>

                <p>ATM withdrawals using foreign cards are widely available in Chinese cities, with daily limits typically $500-$2,000 USD equivalent depending on your home bank and card type. However, ATM withdrawals are impractical for large hospital bills due to daily limits and multiple transaction fees.</p>

